From cec61cef765fc6123b397cc81c093ce0226f457f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Date: Sat, 14 Nov 2026 03:39:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] j604: tune the speakers with our own biquads, fitted to a
 measured target

The J604 speaker graph used the J314 FIRs (the J504 tuning). Replace the
four convolvers with a biquad chain per driver group, fitted to the
frequency response of this machine's macOS speaker processing (speakerID
3, default operating point), measured offline at small signal from a
mono input. Only the response is used; no Apple coefficients, presets or
files.

- Woofers: 12 biquads (three high-passes at 49-77 Hz, the bass lift, two
  low-passes, three dips), 0.18 dB RMS from the target over 37 Hz-2.1 kHz.
- Tweeters: 7 biquads (three high-passes at 1.5-2.6 kHz, three peaks, a
  high shelf), 0.49 dB RMS over 1.46-20 kHz, polarity inverted: each
  side's two-woofer + tweeter sum then follows the target through the
  crossover to 0.21 dB RMS, 100 Hz-16 kHz (with the tweeter in phase the
  sum has a 36 dB hole at 1.6 kHz).
- Level: woofer drive at or below the J314 graph at every frequency up to
  300 Hz (34 dB below it at 40 Hz, where the target high-passes the
  woofers); tweeter peak gain 5.8 dB (J314: 6.7 dB). The midrange is about
  6 dB quieter at the same volume step.
- Left out: crosstalk cancellation (no asahi-audio block does it) and the
  dynamic loudness normaliser; virtual bass stays bankstown.

Unchanged: bankstown, both loudness compensators, the woofer band
compressor and compressor, both true-peak limiters, speakersafetyd. The
1388-sample latency the convolvers declared is now declared by a
zero-delay "delay" node on each path (Delay 0 is a plain copy), so the
graph reports the same latency as before.

Biquads are computed from the stream rate, so the graph is the same at
44.1, 48, 88.2 and 96 kHz, unlike the per-rate FIR files.
